{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789210729,"nonce":"zi2/cOFQAZ8NUrwsz+0u2gE9uTQ=","title":"Cross-module name collision in find_imported_var","message":"Fixed; see git log for the landing commit. Record migrated from session memory 2026-09-12.\n\n\nRoot cause was **not** constructor overloading. It was the\ncross-module import resolver returning classes from the wrong\nmodule. Fix in two places:\n\n- `src/sema/resolve.rs` — `ResolveResult` now carries\n  `module_var_sources: Vec\u003cOption\u003cString\u003e\u003e` parallel to\n  `module_vars`. For each `import \"path\" for Name`, the entry\n  records `Some(path)`. Local declarations: `None`.\n\n- `src/runtime/vm.rs` — new `find_imported_var_from(name, source)`\n  resolves against `engine.modules[source]` directly. The legacy\n  `find_imported_var(name)` is kept as a fallback for the `.wlbc`\n  snapshot path (which doesn't yet serialise sources).\n\n20/20 interpreter runs pass after the fix. Previously ~50% failed.\n\n\nRunning `web.spec.wren` fails ~50% of runs (both interpreter and tiered modes, reproduces with WLIFT_DISABLE_JIT=1 too):\n\n```\nError: Class does not implement 'new(_)'\n 108 │     Expect.that(r.status).toBe(200)\n     │                                ─┬─\nNote: `Class` has no getter or method `new(_)`.\n```\n\nWhen it runs cleanly, all 31/31 tests pass. The error is NOT in the\ntest assertions — it surfaces as a class dispatch miss inside the\ntest runner that was working fine a moment before.\n\n\nThe `Response` class has three overloaded constructors:\n```wren\nconstruct new() { init_(200, {}, \"\") }\nconstruct new(status) { init_(status, {}, \"\") }\nconstruct new(status, body) { init_(status, {}, body) }\n```\n\nOn some module loads, Wren's class-registration picks up only\nsome of the constructors, so `Response.new(200)` is dispatched\nagainst the metaclass `Class` (which doesn't implement `new(_)`)\nrather than Response's own constructor table. The error message\ngives it away — \"`Class` has no getter or method `new(_)`\" —\nit's reporting the metaclass, not the intended target.\n\n\nA minimal class-with-three-ctors reproduction (no imports) runs\nclean 5/5. The flake only appears when loading the large\n`hatch/packages/hatch-web/web.wren` (1100+ lines, 11 classes,\n6 imports). Likely a race / ordering issue during module\ninitialization where the second or third overload isn't\nregistered on some runs.\n\nUnrelated to tiered JIT — WLIFT_DISABLE_JIT=1 still flakes.\n\n\nNone that's clean. The current spec uses `Response.new(200)`\neverywhere (avoiding `Response.new()`) but the flake persists\nwith `new(_)` too. The code itself is correct — when the\nmodule loads cleanly, all 31/31 tests pass.\n\n\n- `src/sema/resolve.rs` — class + method table construction\n- `src/runtime/engine.rs` — module init ordering\n- `src/mir/builder.rs` — method / constructor registration\n\nReduce the bug by: trimming web.wren classes until it stops\nflaking; that'll identify which class's constructors\ncollide or get skipped. Suspects: Session has multi-arity\nstatic methods, Router has `new()` + `new_(prefix)`, Pipeline_\nhas `new_(stack, terminal)`.\n\n\nNot blocking framework development — Phase 2 of @hatch:web\n(Session, Csrf, Static, Flash) is functionally complete.\nWorth investigating before the framework is relied on at\nscale, but logged as a separate bug from the OSR-entry /\ntemplate-tiered flakes.","files":null}]}